window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"06-05","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"06-05-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-01/","title_he":"א – מגורי נכרים בארץ ישראל","title_en":"Gentiles Living in the Land of Israel","body_en":"<p>The great vision of the people of Israel in its Land is that the Land be settled by the people of Israel, that the Temple stand on the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, that all the systems of national life be conducted according to the guidance of the Torah in morality and holiness, and that the people of Israel be a light to the nations, who will come to visit the Land and receive inspiration for the repair of the peoples and the world. As in the words of the prophet (Yeshayahu 2:2-4): \"And it shall come to pass in the end of days that the mountain of the Lord's house shall be established at the top of the mountains, and shall be exalted above the hills, and all the nations shall flow to it. And many peoples shall go and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, to the house of the God of Yaakov, and He will teach us of His ways and we will walk in His paths; for out of Zion shall go forth Torah, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. And He shall judge between the nations and decide for many peoples, and they shall beat their swords into ploughshares and their spears into pruning hooks; nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more.\"</p>\n<p>To realise the vision, the whole Land must be settled by Israel, and only gentiles who wish to be partners in the great vision of Israel will be able to join the people of Israel in the status of a 'ger toshav' (resident alien). Indeed, the road to realising the vision is still long, but we must strive as far as possible to realise it.</p>\n<p>Therefore we must learn the two prohibitions mentioned in the Torah concerning gentiles living in the Land of Israel. The first is a general prohibition that obligates the people of Israel, as it is said (Shemot 23:32-33): \"You shall make no covenant with them, nor with their gods. They shall not dwell in your land, lest they make you sin against Me; for if you serve their gods, it will surely be a snare to you.\" And the second is a particular prohibition directed at each individual, that he not sell land to a gentile, so as not to give them a foothold in the Land, as it is said (Devarim 7:2-4): \"You shall make no covenant with them, and show them no favour (lo teḥonnem). Neither shall you intermarry with them… for he will turn away your son from following Me, that they may serve other gods…\" The great Rishonim and Aḥaronim disagreed on the question of which gentiles these prohibitions were said about. Three principal views were said on this:</p>\n<p>According to the first view, the prohibition \"they shall not dwell in your land\" applies to the seven Canaanite nations alone, with whom the Torah dealt when it commanded the children of Israel as they were about to enter the Land. But the prohibition \"lo teḥonnem\" applies also to other peoples, according to the second or third view below (Rashi, Ra'avad, Semag).</p>\n<p>According to the second view, these prohibitions apply in all generations to idol worshippers or to people who do not keep the seven Noahide mitzvot, regarding whom there is the concern that they will make Israel sin. But one who is fit to be considered a 'ger toshav', that is, one who keeps the seven Noahide mitzvot out of faith in God, the God of Israel, while accepting the sovereignty of the people of Israel over its Land as the Torah commands, is not subject to the prohibition (Kesef Mishneh in his commentary on the Rambam, Mizbe'aḥ Adama). These are the seven Noahide mitzvot: the prohibition of idolatry, the prohibition of forbidden relations, the prohibition of bloodshed, the prohibition of theft and robbery, the prohibition of \"blessing\" the Name (a euphemism, meaning cursing), the prohibition of eating a limb from a living animal, and the obligation to appoint courts that will judge righteously in all the laws between a person and his fellow.</p>\n<p>According to the third view, every gentile who has not been accepted by a beit din into the status of a 'ger toshav', we were commanded that he not dwell in our Land, and that he not be given a foothold in the soil of the Land of Israel. And at a time when the Jubilee is not in force, the batei din have no authority to accept a 'ger toshav', and consequently today, when the Jubilee is not in force, there is no permission to leave gentiles in the Land. The reason for this view is that the Torah wished to guide us to establish a holy people in the Land of Israel, and as long as the gentile has not formally and in an orderly way accepted upon himself to live according to the principles of the Torah of Israel, he may influence the society for the bad (so is the view of the Rambam according to the Maggid Mishneh and the Minḥat Ḥinukh; and so is the view of the Ritva and the Netziv).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The view that the prohibition \"they shall not dwell in your land\" applies only to the seven nations is the view of Rashi, Gittin 45a; Ra'avad, Laws of Idolatry 10:6; Semag, negative commandments 47, 49. However, it seems that according to them too the prohibition \"lo teḥonnem\" applies to all gentiles (like the second or third view). And regarding the view of the Rambam in Laws of Idolatry 10:6 they disagreed. According to the Kesef Mishneh, whoever in practice keeps the seven mitzvot, there is no prohibition to leave him in the Land. And so the Mizbe'aḥ Adama agreed. And this is the second view. However, most poskim understood that according to the Rambam only one who was accepted as a 'ger toshav' by a beit din may live in the Land, and today there is no possibility of accepting a ger toshav. So the Ra'avad understood (though he himself holds otherwise), and so the Maggid Mishneh and the Minḥat Ḥinukh understood. Rav Rabinovitch in Yad Peshuta (Laws of Idolatry 10:7) distinguishes between two kinds of ger toshav: one who was accepted in an orderly way by a beit din and whom we are obligated to sustain, and such a status can be reached only when the Jubilee is in force; and there are gentiles who in practice conduct themselves as a ger toshav, and there is no prohibition that they live in the Land, but there is no mitzva to sustain them.<br>Also holding like the third view are the Ritva, Makkot 9a, and the Kaftor Va-ferach. And so it is implied from Yalkut Shimoni Mishpatim, remez 361. By contrast, from the Mekhilta de-Rabbi Shimon bar Yoḥai, chapter 23:33, the second view is implied, and likewise from the Radak, II Shmuel 24:23, and likewise from the Yere'im, siman 315. As a general rule, the prohibition \"lo teḥonnem\" and \"they shall not dwell in your land\" correspond; so wrote Rav Kook, and so it is explained in Rav Goren's responsum below, siman 1 section 8, and siman 2 responsum 3.<br>This dispute is one of the branches of the dispute over the 'heter mekhira' (sale permit) in Shevi'it. According to the Netziv and several other Aḥaronim, the halakha follows the third view, and therefore it is forbidden to sell the fields to a gentile in the Shemita year. And the supporters of the heter mekhira, among them R. Yitzḥak Elḥanan, the Rav of Kutna and Rav Mohilever, and after them our master Rav Kook, relied in a time of pressing need on the second view, which permits selling fields in the Land of Israel to one who in practice keeps the seven Noahide mitzvot. And even this only in a sale intended for one year, on condition of returning it, and in order to strengthen Jewish settlement in the Land (Peninei Halakha Shevi'it and Yovel 7:6, 5)."}]},{"slug":"06-05-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-02/","title_he":"ב – יחס ההלכה למגורי ערבים במדינת ישראל","title_en":"The Halakha's Attitude to Arabs Living in the State of Israel","body_en":"<p>At the time of the establishment of the State, the great rabbis discussed the status of the Arabs, and the question whether there is a mitzva to act to expel them or to encourage their emigration from the Land.</p>\n<p>According to many of the great poskim, the halakha is that every gentile who is not a 'ger toshav' is forbidden to dwell in our Land, and so in their opinion holds the Rambam (Laws of Idolatry 10:6, like the third view above). Seemingly it follows from this that the Arabs must be expelled or their emigration from the Land encouraged, since they cannot be gerim toshavim, because today, at a time when the Jubilee is not in force, there is no possibility of accepting a 'ger toshav'. However, several rabbis, headed by the Chief Rabbi, Rav Yitzḥak Herzog, explained that the prohibition is to bring into the Land a gentile who comes from abroad: since there is no possibility of accepting a 'ger toshav', there is a mitzva not to bring him in. But the Arabs who already live in the Land, as long as in practice they keep the seven mitzvot and recognise the right of the Jewish people to its Land, there is no mitzva to expel them, and one must treat them with respect and love (below, halakha 5).</p>\n<p>Over the decades that have passed it has become clear that most of the members of the Druze community keep the seven mitzvot in practice, recognise the right of the Jewish people to its Land, and help the people of Israel in its war against its enemies, and consequently they must be treated as gerim toshavim.</p>\n<p>But on the other hand, many of the Arabs living in the Land of Israel do not accept the sovereignty of the people of Israel over its Land. In addition, many of them do not keep the seven Noahide mitzvot, some in that they collaborate with the terrorists who transgress \"You shall not murder\", and some in that they do not act to bring them to justice, according to the seventh mitzva they were commanded, to establish courts (see Rambam, Kings 9:14). It follows, then, that many of them are not considered gerim toshavim, and there is a mitzva to expel them from the Land. And when, for international and moral reasons, there is no possibility of expelling them, at least their emigration must be encouraged. We have no ability today to know what percentage of the Arab population between the sea and the Jordan may be entitled to the title 'ger toshav' and how many are not entitled. For to our sorrow, the governments of Israel abandoned the Arab population in Judea, Samaria and Gaza to the rule of the murder gangs that were brought in and armed by them, and abandoned the Arab citizens of Israel to the influence and control of the elements hostile to Israel, and did not offer them the possibility of taking part in building the State and defending its security against the enemies from outside. Therefore only after we win and expel those who openly fight against us, whether they are within the 'Green Line' or outside it, will we be able to offer the remaining population the true choice: to live here according to the principles of morality and justice in the heritage of Israel, according to the definition of 'ger toshav' including service in the army, or to emigrate to another country.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In passing, I will note that in my humble opinion the Christian population in the Land is closer to being considered gerim toshavim than the Muslims, in that they fight against us less over the Land. And this although in terms of the Muslims' faith they are preferable to the Christians, for there is no idolatry in them, as the Rambam wrote (Laws of Idolatry 9:4, and Forbidden Foods 11:7). And so he wrote in a responsum (448) to R. Ovadia the convert: \"These Ishmaelites are not idol worshippers at all; it has already been cut off from their mouths and their hearts, and they proclaim the unity of God, may He be exalted, fittingly, a unity without blemish.\" And according to the Rambam it is forbidden to do business with the Christians on Sunday, which is their festival day. However, in practice it is accepted to regard most Christians too as those who are not devoted to idolatry, but \"the custom of their fathers is in their hands\" (and Tosafot wrote, Ḥullin 13b, that this is the law also for the gentiles in the Land of Israel), and therefore people are lenient to do business with them on Sunday. And more than this the Me'iri wrote (Bava Kamma 113a, as brought in the Shita Mekubetzet, and Yoma 84b), that their law is like gerim toshavim. And so wrote Maharitz Ḥayot in the essay 'Tiferet Le-Yisrael': \"The Christians who believe in religion and Torah from Heaven… without doubt their laws with us are like a ger toshav… and although they associate something else in their worship, the Ran and Rabbenu Yeruḥam already wrote in the name of Tosafot (Bekhorot 2b, 'shema'), brought in the Rema OḤ siman 156, that gentiles were not commanded concerning association (shittuf)…\" And it is brought in Melumdei Milḥama p. 145, and see further Yad Peshuta by Rav Rabinovitch, Laws of Idolatry 10:7. And the Rambam in his commentary on the beginning of tractate Ḥullin wrote that one should judge favourably those who err at a time when the standing of Israel is not as it should be. And see Teḥumin 2, an article by Rav Gershuni; and there 8, an article by Rav Elisha Aviner; and there 22, p. 68 onward, on the status of the Christians. And there in note 7 is brought the responsum of the Rambam that it is permitted to teach Christians Torah in order to draw them close, whereas the Muslims it is forbidden, because the Christians are closer to accepting our faith, since they believe in the Tanakh, whereas the Muslims deny the Tanakh. And may it be His will that the words of the prophet be speedily fulfilled (Tzefanya 3:9): \"For then I will turn to the peoples a pure language, that they may all call upon the name of the Lord, to serve Him with one consent.\""}]},{"slug":"06-05-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-03/","title_he":"ג – מגורי נכרים מצד מצוות ישוב הארץ","title_en":"Gentiles Living in the Land from the Aspect of the Mitzva of Settling the Land","body_en":"<p>In addition to the question of which gentiles we were commanded should not dwell in our Land, so that we will not be drawn after their ways, when it concerns a national group that may claim sovereignty over the Land, there is a further consideration that they should not dwell in our Land, so that we will be able to fulfil the mitzva of settling the Land. As it is said (Bamidbar 33:53): \"And you shall dispossess the land and dwell in it, for I have given you the land to possess it.\" And Rashi explained: \"And you shall dispossess it of its inhabitants, and then you shall dwell in it, meaning you will be able to endure in it. And if not, you will not be able to endure in it.\" And the Siftei Ḥakhamim explains: \"'Horashtem' is an expression of expulsion.\"</p>\n<p>And there further on (Bamidbar 33:55-56): \"But if you do not dispossess the inhabitants of the land from before you, then those you let remain of them shall be pricks in your eyes and thorns in your sides, and they shall harass you in the land in which you dwell. And it shall come to pass that as I thought to do to them, so I will do to you.\" In addition, those wicked people who have made murder, violence and falsehood the tools of their trade, even though their religion is not defined as idolatry, are considered worse than idol worshippers, and especially when they muster their strength in their war against the Torah of Israel.</p>\n<p>Likewise it is known that all the laws dealing with gentiles living in the Land from the aspect of the prohibition \"they shall not dwell in your land\" concern a situation in which there is no war, or within the framework of limited conflicts. But in war the rules are different: one does not examine each person as an individual, but relates to the whole of the society fighting against us; and then, in addition to the very striving for victory in battle, the rules of \"they shall not dwell in your land\" change too, and if it is possible, the whole population must be expelled, as the State of Israel did at the time of the War of Independence. And only those who are known to have actually supported us must be left, like Raḥav and her family in Yeriḥo.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-05-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-04/","title_he":"ד – הסיבות המונעות את קיום המצווה","title_en":"The Reasons That Prevent Fulfilling the Mitzva","body_en":"<p>For many reasons we are unable to expel the gentiles who do not accept the seven Noahide mitzvot, as the mitzva of the Torah requires. First, the mitzva obligates us at a time when we have the power, but when the hand of the gentiles is strong over us, we are prevented by compulsion from fulfilling it (Rambam, Laws of Idolatry 10:6). Likewise the mitzva of settling the Land does not require us to rely on a miracle and fight alone against the whole world. It is reasonable that today, when the strong countries of the world oppose this, each country with its own reasons, and the security and economic standing of the State of Israel depends on them to a great extent, we are considered as those over whom the hand of the gentiles is strong, and we do not have the power to expel from the Land the Arabs who openly fight against us, and certainly we do not have the power to expel their families and the Arab population that is hostile to us.</p>\n<p>Beyond this, also because of 'the ways of peace' we cannot expel the Arab population that is hostile to us, since such an action would break the peace accepted in international relations, and we find that at times mitzvot are set aside because of 'the ways of peace' (Gittin 59a; Rambam, Gifts to the Poor 1:9).</p>\n<p>Moreover, in recent generations, thanks to the moral influence of the Torah of Israel, we have merited that the nations of the world have accepted upon themselves laws that protect the rights of minorities, and because of desecration of the Name we are forbidden to expel the Arabs who are not in the category of 'ger toshav'. For it is a halakhic rule that there cannot be something forbidden to the children of Noah and permitted to Israel (Sanhedrin 59a), and if according to the laws that the most enlightened of the nations of the world have set, it is forbidden to expel members of a minority population even when they are hostile, the people of Israel too must take this moral position into account as far as possible, all the more so when it concerns binding international conventions.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-05-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-05/","title_he":"ה – המדיניות הראויה למצבנו","title_en":"The Fitting Policy for Our Situation","body_en":"<p>Nevertheless, the basic mitzva concerning \"they shall not dwell in your land\" and \"lo teḥonnem\" has not been annulled, and we are obligated to strive to find the way to fulfil the mitzvot of the Torah concerning gentiles living in the Land within the framework of the moral outlook accepted today. For this we must go deeply into the moral values in a way that will make it understood how fulfilling the mitzva will lead to a just and repaired moral situation for the people of the whole world, and out of this we will find the fitting ways to fulfil the mitzva.</p>\n<p>Apparently the fitting way to advance today towards fulfilling the mitzva is by setting a social and economic policy that will encourage emigration, in peaceful ways, of those who do not identify with the values of the Jewish people.<sup>3</sup> Likewise, it may be that we will yet have to go through wars and crises, and if the aim is before our eyes, we will be able to act so that the result of the wars and crises will be the expulsion of hostile gentiles from our Land.</p>\n<p>Indeed, in the meantime, when in practice we cannot act to expel those who are not entitled to be considered a 'ger toshav', the basic mitzva returns: to treat all creatures with respect and love. For the aim of the mitzva is to establish a society that is entirely a partner in the ideal of the Torah, and not for the sake of hatred of strangers and harming them. Therefore, when for various reasons the mitzva cannot be fulfilled, or it can be fulfilled in a limited way by encouraging emigration, the basic mitzva to treat with respect and love every person who is not known to hold wicked positions or to be hostile to us remains in its place.<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>In any case, finding a deep solution for fulfilling the mitzva and for the challenges of identity and security of the State of Israel will bring healing and blessing to many countries that are coping with similar problems.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"For example, there are different positions concerning the responsibility of the state for the welfare of its residents, both morally and in terms of economic benefit. The capitalist position narrows the responsibility, and the socialist position widens it. In addition, some make most of the rights the state grants conditional on accepting obligations, and some do not make this conditional. When the mitzva stands before our eyes, in the present situation, one should prefer the capitalist position that makes most rights conditional on accepting obligations."},{"n":4,"en":"We find similarly concerning the mitzva of returning a lost object, that it is forbidden to return a lost object to a gentile (Sanhedrin 76b), for this is a mitzva between Israel, who are responsible for one another, and one who returns to a gentile shows that the mitzva is not important in his eyes, since he equates the gentile with a Jew (Rashi). And some say it is because it is forbidden to strengthen the hands of transgressors (Rambam and SA ḤM 266:1). And if there is a concern of desecration of the Name if he does not return it, one is obligated to return it to him (Bava Kamma 113b). Not only this, but one who wishes to return a lost object to a gentile in order to sanctify the name of Heaven, it is permitted and a mitzva (Yerushalmi Bava Metzia 2:5). For the prohibition is to return it to him as an expression of mutual responsibility, or because he strengthens a transgressor; but in order to draw him close to faith, it is permitted and a mitzva (Rambam, Laws of Lost Objects 11:13; SA ḤM 266:1). Similarly we learned that Avraham our father welcomed guests and did kindness with those he thought were idol worshippers (Bava Metzia 86b), although it is forbidden to do kindness with them because of 'lo teḥonnem'; but if the aim is to draw them close, it is permitted. Indeed, on the basis of this foundation we learn that one must indeed check that the kindness is appreciated, and this is not done today; and if it is not appreciated, it should be done only when without it there is a concern of desecration of the Name.<br>On the fitting principled attitude towards the gentiles we learn in Middot Ha-Re'aya, Ahava 5: \"The love of creatures must be alive in the heart and the soul, the love of all humanity especially, and the love of all the peoples, desire for their elevation and their spiritual and material revival; and the hatred must be only of the wickedness and the filth in the world… Wherever we find hints of hatred (towards gentiles), we know clearly that the intention is only to the wickedness, which binds strongly the association of many peoples, also in the present, and especially in days of old, when the filth of the world was more defiled. But we must know that a point of life, light and holiness has never moved from the divine image with which humanity as a whole was graced, and with which every people and tongue was graced, each according to its measure…\" There, Ahava 10: \"The love of creatures needs much care, to widen it to the breadth fitting for it, against the superficiality that appears at first glance through a use that is not complete, from the side of the (mitzvot of the) Torah and from the side of customary morality, as though there are contradictions, or at least indifference, to this love, which must always fill all the chambers of the soul. The highest standing in the love of creatures must be taken by the love of humanity, and it must spread over all of humanity, despite all the differences of opinions, religions and faiths, and despite all the distinctions of races and climates. It is right to go down to the depth of the understanding of the different peoples and groups, to learn as much as possible their character and their qualities, in order to know how to base human love on foundations that come close to action. For only on a soul rich in the love of creatures and the love of humanity can the love of the nation rise up in the pride of its nobility and its spiritual and practical greatness. And the narrowness of vision that causes one to see in everything outside the boundary of the particular nation, even if it is outside the boundary of Israel, only ugliness and impurity, is among the terrible darknesses that cause general destruction to the whole building of spiritual good, whose light every refined soul awaits.\""},{"n":5,"en":"The movements of the left deny the decisive weight of national and religious identity. Accordingly, they support absorbing refugees of a different identity and their right to express their identity publicly, even when it opposes and harms the national identity of the locals. In this way many countries in Europe reached a severe crisis with the Muslim immigrants.<br>The contribution that Judaism can offer the movements of the right in Europe is the adoption of the 'ger toshav' model as a condition for receiving the rights of a citizen and resident; in this way they will be able to preserve the national identity of the countries together with fair and respectful treatment of immigrants. A ger toshav must accept upon himself two principles: first, recognition of the national and religious identity of the people of the place, and bearing the burden of the national challenges in the face of the enemy or of competing cultures. Second, accepting the seven Noahide mitzvot, which include proper moral conduct, including firm opposition to murder and robbery and unreserved support for upholding the law. One who accepts upon himself the rules of ger toshav must be granted full rights, and about him it is said: \"The stranger who dwells with you shall be to you as one born among you, and you shall love him as yourself\" (Vayikra 19:34). However, when there is a concern that absorbing an excessive number of gerim toshavim will blur the identity of the state, the state may limit the number absorbed, even if they are ready to accept upon themselves the principles of ger toshav. But those who are not ready to accept the rules, to respect the culture of the majority and its moral position and not to preach against it in the mosques, and all the more so those who engage in theft, selling drugs, false reporting to the authorities and obstructing the activity of the police, are not entitled to the rights of citizens, and it is fitting to return them respectfully to their countries of origin. It is worth noting that the Jews in the Diaspora conducted themselves towards the countries hosting them as gerim toshavim. And only in the modern era did Jewish people of the left begin to scorn the religion of the people of the place where they lived, and to undermine them by founding revolutionary movements. The State of Israel too suffers from the severe harm done by these Jews."}]},{"slug":"06-05-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-06/","title_he":"ו – סיכום קצר של יסודות איסור נסיגה","title_en":"A Short Summary of the Foundations of the Prohibition of Withdrawal","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to give any territory of the territories of the Land of Israel to gentiles, and even giving one clod of earth is forbidden, and several prohibitions are bound up with this:</p>\n<p><b>a) The mitzva of settling the Land:</b> We are commanded to conquer the Land, as it is said (Bamidbar 33:53): \"And you shall dispossess the land and dwell in it, for I have given you the land to possess it.\" And it is further said (Devarim 11:31): \"For you are crossing the Jordan to come to possess the land which the Lord your God gives you, and you shall possess it and dwell in it.\" And as the Ramban wrote in his additions to the positive commandments, 4, this mitzva applies at all times. And so it is ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (EH 75), and the Pitḥei Teshuva wrote (there, 6) that this is the agreement of all the poskim. And it is explained that the mitzva of settling the Land overrides even danger to the lives of individuals, for according to the Torah we are commanded to conquer the Land, and there is no war without casualties; from here we learn that with regard to the mitzva of settling the Land, the danger to the lives of individuals is not taken into account (Minḥat Ḥinukh 425). And if we are commanded to conquer the Land, all the more so we must not give up parts of it (above 3:1; 3:4).</p>\n<p><b>b) Danger to life:</b> According to what is explained in the Talmud (Eruvin 45a), and as is ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (OḤ 329:6), in every concession, even a small one, to the enemy there is risk, since it gives the enemy an incentive to continue attacking. Therefore they ruled to desecrate Shabbat and to endanger lives in order to fight against enemies who came to steal straw and stubble from a settlement near the border, because if we give up straw and stubble to them, our deterrent power will be harmed, and they will come to conquer settlements and kill people. All the more so one must not concede whole settlements to the enemy (this foundation will be expanded in the next halakha).</p>\n<p><b>c) They shall not dwell in your land:</b> In addition to the general mitzva to possess the Land, the Torah commanded: \"They shall not dwell in your land\" (Shemot 23:33), and the Rambam ruled (Laws of Idolatry 10:6) that at a time when our hand is strong, one must not allow anyone who is not a 'ger toshav' to dwell in our Land. But the poskim disagreed about exactly whom the prohibition applies to. Some say that only a gentile who accepted upon himself before a beit din to keep the seven Noahide mitzvot out of faith in God, the God of Israel, is considered a 'ger toshav' and may live in the Land at a time when our hand is strong. And some say that even if the gentile did not explicitly accept upon himself before a beit din to be a 'ger toshav', if in practice he does not worship idols and keeps the seven Noahide mitzvot, there is no prohibition for him to live in the Land of Israel. And according to their view, decent and good Muslims may live in the Land, for there is no idolatry in their faith. But the Arabs who oppress us certainly do not keep the seven Noahide mitzvot, for they deny God, the God of Israel, support the murderous terrorists and transgress \"You shall not murder\", and do not establish a court to judge the murderers. It follows that according to both views, at a time when our hand is strong, we are forbidden to leave them in the Land, and all the more so we must not give them estates in which they will be able to settle more and more gentiles who transgress the seven Noahide mitzvot (and see above, halakha 1).</p>\n<p><b>d) Lo teḥonnem:</b> The Torah further forbade \"and show them no favour (lo teḥonnem)\" (Devarim 7:2), and the Sages explained that it is forbidden to give gentiles a foothold in the soil of the Land of Israel (Avoda Zara 20a). And this prohibition is the complement of the prohibition \"they shall not dwell in your land\": the prohibition \"they shall not dwell\" applies to the whole of Israel, and the prohibition \"lo teḥonnem\" applies to each individual, that he not sell a house or a field in the Land of Israel to a gentile who is not in the category of a 'ger toshav'. Consequently it is forbidden to give any part of the Land of Israel to Arabs who do not keep the seven Noahide mitzvot properly (above, halakha 1).</p>\n<p><b>e) It shall not be sold in perpetuity:</b> It is said in the Torah (Vayikra 25:23): \"And the land shall not be sold in perpetuity\", and the Ramban learned from this, following the Sages, that one must not sell any land of the lands of the Land of Israel to a gentile, because it will not return in the Jubilee (Ramban, negative commandments 227). All the more so it is forbidden for the representatives of the public to give lands of our forefathers' inheritance to gentiles.</p>\n<p><b>f) Robbing an individual's home:</b> No king or government in the world has the right to uproot a Jew from his home in the Land of Israel. For the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Land as an inheritance to Israel, and every Jew has a portion in the Land, and no government has authority to rob his inheritance and uproot him from the home he bought or built lawfully. Therefore any agreement based on uprooting a Jew from his home is forbidden (see below, 8).<sup>6</sup></p>\n<p><b>g) Desecration of the Name:</b> When the withdrawal comes through compulsion on the part of the nations, if we agree to give up the Land that the Holy One, blessed be He, gave as an inheritance to our forefathers and to us, there is in this a desecration of God's Name, and it is a mitzva to sanctify God and to oppose any compulsion against religion (this foundation will be expanded in halakha 6).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"There is a well-known dispute among the Rishonim (below 6:3) whether 'dina de-malkhuta' (the law of the kingdom) obligates also in the Land of Israel. According to the Ran (Nedarim 28a), only outside the Land does dina de-malkhuta obligate, because there the land belongs to the king, and whoever lives in his land is obligated to obey his laws and rules. But in the Land of Israel, which belongs to all Israel, there is no halakhic obligation to obey the government. And only when it concerns a Jewish government that has the law of a king of Israel is there an obligation to obey its laws. Whereas according to the Rambam and most poskim, in the Land of Israel too the rule applies: 'dina de-malkhuta dina'. For the very public consent to the government grants the government the power to rule and to set laws. But on the foundation of the words of the Ran (and so wrote the Rashba there) there is no dispute, that is, according to all views no government has the right and authority to uproot a Jew from his forefathers' inheritance."}]},{"slug":"06-05-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-07/","title_he":"ז – איסור ויתור לשונאים מחמת סכנת נפשות","title_en":"The Prohibition of Conceding to Enemies Because of Danger to Life","body_en":"<p>An important foundation for the prohibition of withdrawing from parts of the Land of Israel is brought in the Talmud (Eruvin 45a), in a sugya dealing with the laws of Shabbat and danger to life. As is known, it is permitted to desecrate Shabbat for the sake of danger to life, but it is forbidden to desecrate Shabbat for the sake of saving money. Therefore the Sages said that if gentiles came to steal money from towns of Israel, it is forbidden to desecrate Shabbat and go out against them with weapons in order to drive them away. And all this is said about towns that do not sit on the border; but if the gentiles came to steal even something small, like straw and stubble, from settlements near the border, then one must desecrate Shabbat, endanger lives and go out against them with weapons in order to save the straw and stubble. For if we allow them today to steal straw and stubble, tomorrow they will come to plunder settlements and kill people. And so it is ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (OḤ 329:6).</p>\n<p>That is, the Sages discerned in their wisdom that if we concede to the gentiles even straw and stubble in places near the border, we will lose our capacity for deterrence, and consequently the appetite of our enemies will grow, and afterwards they will want to conquer our estates and endanger our lives. Therefore they set the halakha that one must desecrate Shabbat and endanger lives in war in order to protect the property of those who live near the border. According to this it is clear that it is forbidden to concede to the gentiles any part of the territories of Judea, Samaria and the Golan, because every concession of property near the border, and all the more so a concession of territories near the border, will increase the appetite of our enemies, and the matter may, heaven forbid, bring the next war nearer.</p>\n<p>Indeed, had all the military experts risen and said that if we withdraw we will indeed achieve true peace, perhaps we would have said that the times have changed and today there is no danger to life in withdrawal. But in practice many of the military experts hold that every withdrawal will endanger the State of Israel, and therefore we have only the words of the Sages, and it is forbidden to concede even straw and stubble in the towns on the border, and all the more so one must not give parts of the Land of Israel into the hands of our enemies.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"The Admor Rabbi Menaḥem Mendel Schneerson zt\"l returned to this foundation many times. And Rav Ovadia Yosef ruled that for the sake of saving lives it is permitted to withdraw from regions of the Land of Israel, and Rav Yisraeli, in Ḥavat Binyamin vol. 1, 13, refuted his words. And see above 3:4. And it has already been proven after the Oslo Accords that those who said that the withdrawal would cause more dangers were right. In principle Rav Yisraeli's view was that if it is clear that through withdrawal Israeli sovereignty will be strengthened in the other regions of the Land, it is permitted to withdraw. However, in practice the withdrawals from places that were under our control, such as Sinai and parts of Judea, Samaria and Gaza, only caused increased pressure and terror upon us. And see the next halakha and note."}]},{"slug":"06-05-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-08/","title_he":"ח – איסור נסיגה מדין חילול השם","title_en":"The Prohibition of Withdrawal Because of Desecration of the Name","body_en":"<p>The Rambam, in the Laws of the Foundations of the Torah, chapter 5, explains that there is a mitzva to sanctify the Name, that is, a Jew must be ready to give his life for the sanctification of God: if gentiles came and commanded him, \"Go and kill your fellow, and if you do not, we will kill you\", he must die and not transgress \"You shall not murder\". And so is the law with all three severe transgressions: forbidden relations, bloodshed and idolatry; because of their great severity, there is no point to life after so terrible a sin, and it is better for a person to die rather than transgress one of these three transgressions.</p>\n<p>But regarding the other mitzvot of the Torah the law is different. If, for example, they seized a Jew and threatened him that if he does not desecrate Shabbat or eat pork they will kill him, he must desecrate Shabbat or eat the pork rather than be killed, because it is said: \"and he shall live by them\", and not that he die by them.</p>\n<p>However, it is important to know that all this is on condition that those gentiles compel the Jew to transgress the mitzvot for their own benefit; but if their aim is to make him abandon his religion and to harm his faith, then if the matter is done in the presence of ten Jews, he must be killed and not transgress. For it no longer concerns a private transgression alone; rather the private transgression represents the whole Torah, and one who transgresses it is as though he transgresses the whole Torah and desecrates God, and therefore the halakha was ruled that he should sanctify God and be killed rather than transgress.</p>\n<p>On this basis our teacher and master Rav Tzvi Yehuda Ha-Kohen Kook zt\"l ruled that the prohibition of withdrawing from regions of the Land of Israel becomes far more severe, for there is in it also desecration of God's Name, and consequently it obligates at the level of \"be killed rather than transgress\". And this is because the whole Tanakh is full of divine promises that the Land of Israel belongs to the people of Israel, and the whole world knows this. And the Arabs, by contrast, fight against the mitzvot of the Torah and the divine promise. The proof of this is that there are many Arab countries that have no border with us, and nevertheless they fight against us. This is nothing other than a religious war of our enemies against our faith.</p>\n<p>If so, withdrawal from the Land of Israel involves desecration of God's Name of the most severe level, for it is done by the public, and the sin of an individual is not like the sin of the public, done before the eyes of all the nations. And especially in so famous a mitzva as settling the Land, for every gentile who has read the Tanakh knows that the Holy One, blessed be He, promised the Land of Israel to His people. And when he sees that Jews give up regions of the Land and disown the divine promise, there is no greater desecration of God's Name than this. And to prevent desecration of God's Name we must be ready to fight with self-sacrifice for the defence of the Land, rather than give up any part of our holy Land, which the Creator of heaven and earth gave us as an inheritance.</p>\n<p>Indeed, it seems that although this obligation is in the category of \"be killed rather than transgress\", in a situation in which we have no chance of winning there is no mitzva to fight, since in any case there is no possibility of being killed and not transgressing; for even if we are killed, we will transgress, and the Land will fall into the hands of gentiles and the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, will be desecrated before the eyes of the nations. But when there is a possibility of keeping our holy estates in the hands of Israel, there is an obligation to fight with self-sacrifice, so that the Land will be in our hands as the Torah commands. And blessed is God, who in His great mercy and kindness returned us to our Land and gave us strength and might, until we have the ability to hold all the regions of our holy Land that are in our hands; and therefore we must guard all its parts with self-sacrifice.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"In my humble opinion, after my teacher and master the Ratziya raised the question to the level of sanctification of the Name, since he saw in it a religious war, the discussion of whether withdrawal will contribute to the settlement of the Land in the other regions of the Land, and whether the withdrawal will bring about the saving of lives, has become superfluous. There are two reasons for this: halakhic and practical. In halakha we are already commanded in this with \"be killed rather than transgress\", and as long as the people of Israel is able to withstand this, there is no longer room for considerations of benefit. At the same time, reality too fits this, because if it concerns a religious war, the enemies will not be satisfied with any compromise, but will always strive to impose their religion and to prevent those who do not submit to their religion from being in territory that in their view should be Muslim. In addition, one must know that in Islam the attitude to war is not one of after the fact; rather war is one of the six mitzvot of Islam. Not as with us, where war is an exceptional phenomenon and we fight only when there is necessity; with them war is a natural and desirable thing as long as there are people in the world who do not accept their religion. The only way to \"exempt\" them from their \"mitzva\" is to show them that they are under compulsion and do not have the power to fight and win. Therefore every withdrawal only increases their energy to continue fighting by means of regular war or terror, in order to achieve the religious aims to which they are committed. Thus the halakha connected to sanctification of God and the realistic outlook correspond completely.<br>See further the book of Rav Yaakov Ziesberg, 'Naḥalat Yaakov' vol. 2, pp. 437-480, a whole chapter explaining the intention of the Ratziya in his determination that the prohibition of withdrawal is in the category of \"be killed rather than transgress\". And what I have explained in his words is like the view of my father and teacher. That is, the situation in which a Jew will actually be called upon to be killed rather than transgress is when he is practically required to bring about the withdrawal. For example, if gentiles threaten the prime minister that if he does not sign a concession they will kill him, he should be killed rather than transgress. And see below in Rav Goren's responsa, siman 8, and in the words of the Ratziya there, and in the note."}]},{"slug":"06-05-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-09/","title_he":"ט – החלטת ממשלה אינה מחייבת בזה","title_en":"A Government Decision Does Not Obligate in This","body_en":"<p>As a general rule, the decisions of the Knesset and the government of Israel are binding, for 'dina de-malkhuta dina' (the law of the kingdom is law), and according to most poskim this rule applies also in the Land of Israel (Rambam; SA ḤM 369:6). And even according to those who hold that 'dina de-malkhuta' does not apply in the Land of Israel (the Ran), this is said about a government of gentiles; but a Jewish government has at least the authority of the 'leaders of the town' (see Tzitz Eliezer 16:50). And some say that a Jewish government that has been accepted by Israel has a kind of authority of the kingdom of Israel. Therefore the decisions of the government and the Knesset are binding, both regarding paying taxes and regarding traffic, building and commercial regulations (as explained below 6:1-5).</p>\n<p>But one significant limitation stands before the law of the kingdom: any law that contradicts the mitzvot of the Torah has no binding force. For it was said to Yehoshua bin Nun that as the leader of Israel he has authority to issue instructions, and every man who rebels against his word will be punished and put to death; nevertheless, in the continuation of the verse his authority is qualified by the expression \"only be strong and of good courage\" (Yehoshua 1:18). That is, Yehoshua's regulations are valid on condition that he fulfils the verse (ibid. 7): \"Only be strong and very courageous, to observe to do according to all the Torah which Moshe My servant commanded you; turn not from it to the right hand or to the left, that you may prosper wherever you go.\" But if he issues instructions contrary to the Torah, his words are void, and a Jew should not obey him (Sanhedrin 49a). And so the Rambam ruled (Laws of Kings 3:9) that if the king decreed to annul a mitzva, one should not obey him, because the king himself is a servant of the King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He.</p>\n<p>And if this is said about a king who was appointed by a prophet like Moshe our teacher, and the Sanhedrin and the people of Israel accepted him upon themselves, all the more so no king or government whatsoever can issue instructions contrary to the mitzvot of the Torah. And if, for example, any government enacts a law that would require desecration of Shabbat, or eating forbidden foods such as pork, or sets a law to uproot Jews from their Land and thereby annul the mitzva of settling the Land, these laws will have no validity, and the obligation will rest on all Israel to oppose the carrying out of the invalid laws.</p>\n<p>Therefore, even if a government comes and forbids Jews to settle in one of the regions of the Land of Israel, its instruction has no validity, because its instruction contradicts the mitzva of settling the Land. However, if Jews came and asked to settle in a certain place, the government has authority to determine that one should not settle there but only near it, when the reasons are public ones, for example that on the place where they wanted to settle a road is planned to be paved or trees planted and the like. But if it says not to settle in a whole region of the Land because it does not agree that there should be Jews there, its instruction contradicts the mitzvot of the Torah and the promise that the Holy One, blessed be He, promised the Land of Israel to the people of Israel, and its instruction has no validity.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Rav Goren further ruled (below in his responsa, siman 7), on the basis of the Rambam (Laws of Kings 1:4) and the Sefer Ha-Ḥinukh (mitzva 498), that a government that seized power with the help of Arab votes, its instructions have no validity. And as long as the majority of Jews do not support it, it has no authority to determine and instruct in any field touching the Jewish people and its right to hold its Land. And so answered Rav Aharon Soloveichik, that it is forbidden to take part in withdrawal and evacuation, and one who takes part transgresses the prohibition \"You shall not stand by the blood of your fellow\" (Gilyon Rabbanei Yesha 15)."}]},{"slug":"06-05-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-10/","title_he":"י – איסור להשתתף בפעולות המסייעות לנסיגה","title_en":"The Prohibition of Taking Part in Actions That Assist Withdrawal","body_en":"<p>According to what we have learned, that it is forbidden to transfer parts of the Land of Israel to the control of gentiles, it is also forbidden to assist in the expulsion of Jews and the destruction of settlements. And so ruled the Chief Rabbi of Israel (5733-5743), Rav Shlomo Goren zt\"l, and so ruled Rav Shaul Yisraeli zt\"l and Rav Neria zt\"l, and the Chief Rabbi of Israel (5743-5753), Rav Avraham Shapira zt\"l. And so, even before that, my teacher and master Rav Tzvi Yehuda Kook zt\"l ruled many times (and see below, Rav Goren's responsa, siman 5, and Rav Shapira's responsum, siman 12; and see Igrot Ha-Re'aya, letter 237).</p>\n<p>A further question was raised before the Union of Rabbis for the People of Israel and the Land of Israel, which united hundreds of rabbis and was headed by Rav Avraham Shapira zt\"l (Chief Rabbi of Israel 5743-5753): whether the law of a military camp is like the law of a settlement.<sup>10</sup> And in the month of Tammuz 5755 a responsum was written, and this is its wording:</p>\n<p>\"Following the rulings given at the time by the Council of the Chief Rabbinate and other rabbis concerning the prohibition of evacuating parts of the Land of Israel, Judea, Samaria and Gaza and the Golan, we were asked whether it is permitted to take part and help in the evacuation of a military camp or military installation located in an area populated by Arabs in the territories of the Land of Israel.</p>\n<p><b>a.</b> We determine that there is a Torah prohibition to evacuate IDF camps and to transfer the place to the control of gentiles, since there is in this the annulment of a positive mitzva and also danger to life and danger to the existence of the State.</p>\n<p><b>b.</b> It seems simple that in territory where the IDF is present and rules, there the mitzva of settling the Land of Israel is fulfilled, as the Ramban wrote, which includes also \"to conquer it and not to leave it in the hands of others among the nations\"; and from territory from which the IDF withdraws there is 'the yoke of the gentiles', and this is included in the annulment of the aforementioned positive mitzva. In addition, there is in this today a risk to lives of Israel, and also danger to the existence of the State, and there is in this the matter of \"You shall not stand by the blood of your fellow\" (Vayikra 19:16).</p>\n<p><b>c.</b> A permanent military camp too is itself a Jewish settlement in every respect; uprooting it and leaving it in the hands of gentiles is included in uprooting a settlement from the Land of Israel, which is forbidden according to the law.</p>\n<p><b>d.</b> And therefore, in answer to the question, it is clear and simple that it is forbidden for any Jew to take part in any act of assistance to the evacuation of a settlement, camp or installation, as is ruled in the Rambam (Laws of Kings 3:9), that even if the king commanded to transgress the words of the Torah, one does not obey him…\"<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"In the month of Tammuz 5755, following the decisions of the minority government to evacuate military bases in Judea, Samaria and Gaza, a question arose among soldiers: must they carry out the decree, or must they refuse orders so as not to take part in the work of evacuation? I contacted our teacher and master the Gaon Rav Avraham Shapira zt\"l, Rosh Yeshiva of 'Merkaz Ha-Rav' and Chief Rabbi of Israel (5743-5753), and at his request I convened a gathering of a number of important rabbis, representatives of the Union of Rabbis for the People of Israel and the Land of Israel. At the end of the gathering this ruling was published. I did not manage to raise this question before the Chief Rabbi of Israel and of the IDF, Shlomo Goren zt\"l, who had passed away before that, on 24 Ḥeshvan 5755.<br>Among the partners in the halakhic ruling were: my father and teacher Rav Zalman Barukh Melamed, Rav Naḥum Eliezer Rabinovitch, Rav Dov Lior, Rav Ḥayim Druckman, Rav Eliezer Waldman, Rav Ḥayim Steiner, Rav Daniel Shilo, and many more. See below, siman 13, for Rav Rabinovitch's reasons for the halakhic ruling."},{"n":11,"en":"The continuation of the wording of the published ruling is: \"<b>e.</b> The IDF has never put its soldiers in a situation in which they were forced to act against their religious, moral or national conscience. We call on the government and the army leadership not to put the soldiers in a situation in which they must agonise between their loyalty to the values on which their way of life is built and the orders of the army. <b>f.</b> We turn to the government and to the one at its head not to lend a hand to the division of the nation and of the IDF, and to strengthen with all their powers the unity of Israel at this difficult hour.\"<br>In an appendix to the ruling it was published: \"The IDF is precious and holy in our eyes, and as its name is, so is it: its role is to defend the Land of Israel and to guard the lives of the people, and not to do actions opposite to its purpose that endanger Jews. Besides the halakhic ruling that it is forbidden to evacuate territories of the Land of Israel, half the nation too is against this agreement; opinions are divided, and so too opinions are divided among the army commanders themselves. It is not moral to compel soldiers to act against their conscience, not only religious but national and moral. This demand of them is a moral crime and is not legal. We hope that the army commanders will allow every soldier to act according to his religious conscience. The Lord will give strength to His people; the Lord will bless His people with peace.\" (The wording of the appendix is according to a draft written by Rav Neria zt\"l.)"}]},{"slug":"06-05-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-11/","title_he":"יא – האיסור קיים גם כשהסירוב לא ימנע נסיגה","title_en":"The Prohibition Applies Even When Refusal Will Not Prevent Withdrawal","body_en":"<p>Even when it is clear to a soldier that if he refuses to take part in the withdrawal and the expulsion of Jews, there will be other soldiers who will carry out the withdrawal and expel Jews, the soldier is forbidden to carry out this order. For even when the general situation is corrupt, there is no permission for an individual to be a partner to the sins the public commits. And every person who with his own hands expels Jews from their homes becomes a partner to several severe Torah prohibitions.<sup>12</sup> He becomes a partner, heaven forbid, to breaking the mitzva of settling the Land, which is weighed against all the mitzvot, and to the terrible desecration of God's Name in this; and he also transgresses the prohibition \"lo teḥonnem\", the prohibition \"they shall not dwell in your land\", the prohibition \"and the land shall not be sold in perpetuity\", and robbery of the settler's property. And if he is a partner in evacuating a camp, he also transgresses the prohibition \"You shall not stand by the blood of your fellow\".</p>\n<p>And if someone asks: what is the benefit of refusing an order if it will not save the Land of Israel? We will answer that even when we do not have the power to prevent the public from sinning, each and every one of us is commanded to guard the purity of his life and the cleanness of his hands. Even in a place where everyone is a thief, the mitzva rests on the individual not to steal. And even if all the others commit violence and robbery and he has no power to influence them to improve their way, he must remain stubborn, alone and righteous.</p>\n<p>This is the foundation learned from the mitzva \"You shall surely rebuke your fellow, and not bear sin because of him\" (Vayikra 19:17). The essence of the mitzva is to rebuke the sinner in order to prevent him from the sin. And as long as there is some chance that the sinner will listen to the rebuke, there is a Torah obligation to rebuke him. However, even when there is no chance that the sinner will listen to the rebuke, at the very least one must express a position, that this deed is a sin and it is forbidden to do it; all the more so it is forbidden to take part in carrying out the sin (see Rema OḤ 608:2, and MB and Bei'ur Halakha there).</p>\n<p>This standing firm for the truth and the refusal to take part in the sin are of the greatest importance, for the sake of truth and for the sake of history, so that in all generations they will know that not everyone erred, and in the merit of those righteous ones the ember will be preserved until the repair of the world. As it is said (I Melakhim 19:18): \"Yet I have left seven thousand in Israel, all the knees that have not bowed to the Ba'al, and every mouth that has not kissed him.\" And so in the time of Gidon, through the three hundred men who had not grown accustomed to kneeling to the Ba'al, he defeated the whole camp of Midian, and a great salvation was wrought through them (Shoftim 7).</p>\n<p>One who merits to guard the way of the eternal truth is truly free, as the Sages said (Avot 6:2): \"There is no free person except one who occupies himself with Torah\", because he is bound and faithful to the eternal principles. He cannot be influenced by media pressures, nor by public opinion, nor by other temptations or intimidations. And so, throughout all the generations, the righteous guarded their personal freedom and were a moral compass for the whole world. And only in their merit will the world, in the end, reach the way of truth. Indeed it is true that most people can be bought with temptations and intimidations, but the people of Israel as a whole always knew how to be faithful to its eternal principles. Even when many of the children of Israel sinned, the people of Israel knew in the end to accept the way of the righteous who did not sin, for they represented the whole of Israel, and in their merit the people of Israel continued on its way.</p>\n<p>When a people is bound to absolute and eternal values, its existence too is absolute and eternal, and all the winds in the world will not move it from its place. And since the people of Israel as a whole is bound with self-sacrifice to divine and eternal values, therefore it lives and endures, and all the troubles and exiles have not consumed it. Moreover, not only does it continue to exist, but its value-laden moral vitality continues to pulse and ferment. But the other nations, which were bound to values that are not absolute, just as the values that guided their lives were relative and limited, so too their existence was relative and limited, and they passed away and vanished. May it be His will that the special quality of Israel be revealed, and the divine values become the heritage of all, and the verse be fulfilled in us (Yeshayahu 60:21): \"And your people shall all be righteous; they shall inherit the land for ever.\"</p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"There is a well-known halakha that whoever assists another person to commit a transgression, in a situation in which without his assistance he could not have committed the transgression, for example one who hands a nazir a cup of wine from the other side of the river, the one who assists the nazir to transgress violates a Torah prohibition: \"and before the blind you shall not put a stumbling block\" (Vayikra 19:14; Avoda Zara 6b). And if even without his assistance he could have committed the transgression, for example the nazir could have taken the cup of wine himself, the one who assists transgresses a rabbinic prohibition of \"assisting in a transgression\". And some say that in this too he transgresses a Torah prohibition. However, here according to all views it cannot be said that the prohibition is only rabbinic, because every soldier who with his own hands evacuates Jews from their homes commits a transgression with his own hands."}]},{"slug":"06-05-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-12/","title_he":"יב – האם לסרב בפומבי","title_en":"Whether to Refuse Publicly","body_en":"<p>There is a further question on this subject: is it preferable for soldiers to announce in advance and publicly that they will not assist in withdrawal, or is it enough that in practice they do not assist the transgression, but it is preferable that they not announce it in advance?</p>\n<p>Some hold that a public announcement of refusal may harm the army, since the army rests on the norm of obeying orders, and public refusal may harm its cohesion. Therefore it is preferable that the soldier not announce it publicly, and only if, heaven forbid, he reaches such a situation should he turn to his commander and tell him that he does not agree to take part in so severe a transgression.</p>\n<p>In 5765, at the time of the destruction of the settlements in Gush Katif and northern Samaria, the view of our teacher and master Rav Avraham Shapira zt\"l, and with him other rabbis, was that it is desirable for the soldiers to announce it publicly as soon as possible. The reason is because of the mitzva of rebuke, and because of the chance that the announcement of many soldiers, together with other public activities, might bring about the cancellation of the decree of expulsion and withdrawal. As for the claim that public refusal may cause severe harm to the IDF, to the point that it will not be able afterwards to fight the enemy properly, one must answer that this claim needs proof. Where have we found that refusal to act against civilians disintegrates an army? And the mitzva of rebuke and the obligation to try to thwart this severe public transgression cannot be set aside by unfounded concerns.</p>\n<p>In his time, Rav Shlomo Goren zt\"l too, who was the Chief Rabbi of the IDF and afterwards of the State of Israel, was not concerned about this, and held that it is fitting for the soldiers to announce publicly that they will refuse orders. And only for the sake of soldiers who want to advance in military service to officer rank and are concerned that an early declaration will harm their plans did he agree that they should not announce anything; but in no way did he agree to permit them to give their commanders the impression that they agree to carry out the order; he only agreed that they should remain silent and not answer (below in his responsa, siman 5, and see the note there).<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"The words of my teacher and master Rav Shapira were said clearly and forcefully in 5765 regarding the government's plan to destroy the settlements of the Gaza coast and four settlements in northern Samaria. At first they were said to Rav Tropp, with agreement that they would be published; afterwards he repeated his words several more times. And see below, siman 12, the decisive words he wrote a week before the destruction of Gush Katif. Before that, at the time of the Oslo Accords, the government's plans concerned only the evacuation of army bases, and regarding them this question was not made explicit. And I wrote in Gilyon Rabbanei Yesha no. 25 of the month of Tammuz 5755, in explanation of the halakhic ruling of the Union of Rabbis (mentioned above in halakha 8), and in it I wrote that because of the doubt whether public refusal will harm the cohesion of the IDF, \"sit and do nothing\" is preferable, and therefore it is better to refuse in practice but not to multiply declarations about it. I published these words afterwards in Peninei Halakha vol. 3 p. 298, from 5755. It is fitting to add that already then there were rabbis who held that the refusal should be public, and on the other hand there were rabbis who were concerned for the cohesion of the IDF. However, it must be noted that according to what I heard then from the great rabbis, they were not concerned about public refusal; rather, according to the situation of that period, they did not think there was great importance to the public declaration on the part of soldiers and officers, and therefore they did not insist on their view against the concerns some rabbis raised. And so I wrote that because of the doubt, \"sit and do nothing is preferable\".<br>And Rav Goren too agreed to this, as was published in Gilyon Rabbanei Yesha no. 14. And his words are brought in the responsa below, siman 5; and see there in the note on the background to this. In any case, the position I published then in Gilyon Rabbanei Yesha, that the refusal should not be made public and blatant, did indeed reduce tension in the army, because the officers exempted the soldiers who wanted to refuse from taking part in the evacuation of camps or outposts. Moreover, the Chief of Staff at that time, Amnon Lipkin-Shahak, told us in a conversation that this was the instruction to the officers, to avoid confrontation with soldiers who would want to refuse, and only in a place where there was a commander \"who is not wise\", in his words, was a problem created. And even then, when the case reached the higher ranks, they hid the \"problem\" and exempted almost all the soldiers who refused from punishment.<br>In 5765 it appeared that there was a chance that the advance announcement of soldiers, together with other factors, could cancel the decree of expulsion, and consequently there is in it the obligation of \"You shall surely rebuke your fellow\". In addition, the transgression in 5765 was more severe: the destruction of settlements and the expulsion of Jews (and not only the evacuation of camps). And on the other hand, it was not proven at all that public refusal to carry out an order that does not belong to the role of the IDF would harm its strength. On the contrary, there is room to say that precisely public refusal will preserve the IDF in the long term as one whose role is to defend against the enemy and not other roles. And so Rav Shapira zt\"l ruled. And one should add that if many soldiers are forced to do things contrary to their faith, in the long term they will not be able to feel identification with the IDF, and precisely this may disintegrate the IDF. Therefore I changed my position from \"sit and do nothing\" to \"rise and act\": it is better that every soldier announce publicly that he will not take part in the severe prohibition. And only for officers and policemen for whom this is their livelihood did I suggest hiding their opinion and finding ways to evade carrying out the prohibition.<br>In practice, precisely those who announced in advance that they would refuse were not put to the test, and the army made efforts not to send them to the tasks of expulsion and destruction. Whereas those who hid their position were put to the test, and some failed. And may God help that in the merit of the knees that did not bow to the order, the IDF will return to fulfil its purposes, to fight the wars of the Lord for the sake of the people and the Land."}]},{"slug":"06-05-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-05-13/","title_he":"יג – הריסת בתים או ישוב בצו בית משפט","title_en":"Demolishing Houses or a Settlement by Court Order","body_en":"<p>To our sorrow we sometimes encounter the action of demolishing Jewish houses in Judea and Samaria by decision of a court, and the question stands before us whether there is in this a breach of the mitzva of settling the Land, or whether this is in the category of 'dina de-malkhuta dina' (below 6:1). The foundation that determines the law is the reason for the demolition, and there are three situations: a) when it concerns religious coercion, this is in the category of \"be killed rather than transgress\"; b) when the legal reason for the demolition is understood, the demolition is permitted; c) an intermediate situation, in which the direct reason is legal but the underlying reason is harm to the settlement of the Land. And we will detail further:</p>\n<p>a) If the demolition is because in the view of the government or the court a Jew is forbidden to live in a certain region of the Land of Israel, then it concerns religious coercion, and there is an obligation to rise up against this in the category of \"be killed rather than transgress\". Indeed, this instruction in practice is very complex, and in most cases it is far from application (above, halakha 7 and note 6), and would that we never need to clarify this terrible question in practice. But certainly it concerns a very sharp struggle bound up with self-sacrifice.</p>\n<p>b) On the other hand, when a Jew wilfully builds his house on a road, or on an area designated for a public garden, or on the land of a private person, whether Jewish or not Jewish, there is no prohibition to demolish his house; on the contrary, in this way proper orders of government are preserved. Likewise, when the court decides that a certain person built his house by robbery on another person's land, whether Jewish or gentile, there is no prohibition to demolish the house. And even if it seems that the court erred in its decision, as long as it concerns the accepted errors of human beings, still \"dina de-malkhuta dina\". And about such things the Sages said: \"even if unwitting, even if wilful, even if mistaken\".</p>\n<p>c) And there are intermediate situations, in which on the one hand there is no principled decision against the right of Jews to live in a certain region of the Land, and the governmental or legal demolition decision is based on procedures and regulations. But on the other hand, it is reasonable that the decision to demolish the house stems from a position that denies the right of Jews to live in that region of the Land. For example, when in every similar case elsewhere in the Land they do not order the demolition of houses but their regularisation. Or for example, when every claim of an Arab is accepted, whereas a claim of a Jew is always rejected.</p>\n<p>In such a case, the question returns to the doorstep of the government, which has the ability to build additional houses in that area. If the government orders the building of many houses in place of the houses that are about to be demolished, it shows thereby that it does not act out of opposition to the settlement of the Land, and the decision on the demolition returns to being permitted, in the category of 'dina de-malkhuta dina'. Indeed, when one finds an injustice in the decision, it is desirable to arouse public debate against it, but one should not oppose its execution.</p>\n<p>But if the government does not use its power to build, then it is reasonable that the demolition stems from a desire to harm the mitzva of settling the Land. Indeed, this is still not in the category of public religious coercion, since the reasons are legal and local. But since the action blatantly contradicts the mitzvot of the Torah, a policeman, soldier or citizen should not respect or obey such an instruction, and it is even a mitzva to rise up against it in all the ways accepted in the free and moral world. But one should not sharpen the struggle beyond this, since it does not concern explicit religious coercion.</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
